@page "/desktop-sales"
@using System.Globalization
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization
@using ShopInventory.Web.Features.DesktopCreditNotes
@using ShopInventory.Web.Features.DesktopSalesDay
@using ShopInventory.Web.Services
@* Manager is here because the credit-note API already allows it and this was the only page
   a credit could be raised from. *@
@attribute [Authorize(Roles = "Admin,Cashier,Manager")]
@inject IDesktopIntegrationService DesktopService
@inject IReportExportService ExportService
@inject INotificationHubService HubService
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject ISnackbar Snackbar
@inject IAuditService AuditService
@inject ILogger<DesktopSales> Logger
@implements IDisposable
@rendermode InteractiveServer

<PageTitle>Desktop Sales - Shop Inventory</PageTitle>

@* Built from `Desktop Sales.dc.html` in the "Nocturne Dark Ops Redesign" design
   project. Styles live in wwwroot/css/operations.css (linked from App.razor),
   shared with the other five operational pages — see that file's header for why
   an imported design joined the derived sheet rather than taking one of its
   own. `ops` is the scope; `ops-ds` is this page's own half of it. The 542-line
   inline <style> block is gone.

   **The design's row selection is built now, against a different verb.** It
   drew a checkbox per row and a "Consolidate 4" button — consolidating a chosen
   subset — and that stayed unbuilt while `TriggerConsolidationAsync()` took no
   selection, and it still takes only a date: checkboxes that did not decide what gets consolidated would have
   been a lie about what the button does.

   `POST sales/post-batch` is the selective endpoint that was missing, so the
   column exists and carries the verb the API actually offers. **Posting is not
   consolidating**, and the two must not be confused by anyone reading this
   page: consolidating is the end-of-day run that folds a day's legacy-desktop
   sales into one invoice per customer, while posting sends the chosen till, van
   and vending sales to SAP as one invoice each — the same thing the background
   pass does every minute, asked for now. So the hero keeps both buttons, and
   only a sale the API says is postable gets a checkbox.

   **The design's second revision** brought the lane strip above the counts
   rather than below them, the counts as a four-up grid, a hover cue on a row
   that opens a drawer, and a drawer that states the document — a headline
   amount, the fiscal block the device signed, and a trail.

   **The third** is almost entirely one thing: the filter surface. A search box,
   a fourth period, a collapsible panel of chip groups that take several values
   at once and carry the count each of them would return, an amount window, a
   tender comparison, a row of pills naming what is set, a sort, and a
   rows-per-page control. Two of those — the multi-value chips and their counts —
   could not be drawn against the API this page had: it filtered on one
   warehouse, one status and one channel, sorted on nothing, and counted nothing.
   So `GET sales` grew the plural filters, the amount window, the tender
   comparison, a sort and — behind `includeFacets`, because five grouped counts
   are not a till's business — the per-group counts the chips carry. Every one of
   them is proved in DesktopSalesFilterSurfaceTests.

   **The counts are what makes the panel worth having**, and they are counted
   with each group's own selection lifted. A chip says what pressing it would
   give, not what is already showing: counted the other way, every unpicked chip
   in a group that has a pick reads 0 and the whole panel looks like a dead end.

   **Warehouse, tender and channel are drawn from the data**, not from a list
   this page keeps. The tenders are whatever the tills have actually written and
   the design's four guesses (Cash, Swipe, Transfer, On account) are not them —
   this app takes Cash, Swipe, EcoCash and InnBucks — so a chip exists where a
   sale exists, and nowhere else. A value that is selected but has nothing left
   to match keeps its chip so it can be unpicked.

   Its remaining per-sale actions are still not drawn, and for the original
   reason. "Consolidate now" in a drawer about one invoice is not Post to SAP:
   posting sends this sale to SAP as its own invoice, consolidating closes the
   whole day. There is still no endpoint that excludes a sale from the close,
   and no receipt PDF is kept for a desktop sale — which is also why the
   design's selection bar reads "Post N to SAP" where it drew "Consolidate
   selected" and "Exclude". The bar itself is the design's, and it is where the
   count belongs: it states what is about to reach SAP, immediately above the
   rows it is about to send.

   Four places where the design assumed data this page does not have:

   - Its status lanes come from the end-of-day report's consolidation counts
     rather than an invented per-device lifecycle, and its per-site value bars
     became per-customer, because `BusinessPartnerSummaries` is the breakdown
     the report returns.
   - Its drawer prints a raised time and a fiscalized time. A desktop sale
     carries `CreatedAt` and nothing else, so one stamp is stated once.
   - Its trail ends at "Posted to ledger" with a journal number. There is no
     ledger posting time on this row. There is a SAP document number, by two
     routes that write different columns — see `trail` — and that is what the
     last step states.
   - Its "fiscal signature" is a line of text. The signature is a URL, so it is
     drawn as the scannable thing it is: the QR panel is where that part of the
     design landed, with the verification code and the same URL as a link beside
     it. *@

<div class="ops ops-ds">

    <div class="ops-hero">
        <div class="ops-hero-text">
            <div class="ops-ds-kicker-row">
                <span class="ops-kicker">Offline fiscalization</span>
                <span class="ops-ds-kicker-rule"></span>
                @* The dot animates only while the hub is up. A still dot is the
                   disconnected state, not an animation that failed to start. *@
                <span class="ops-ds-live">
                    <span class="ops-ds-live-dot @(HubService.IsConnected ? "" : "is-off")"></span>
                    @(HubService.IsConnected ? "Live" : "Offline")
                </span>
            </div>
            <h1>Desktop Sales</h1>
            <p>
                Invoices raised in the desktop apps and fiscalized on the local device.
                They stay here until the day is consolidated into the back office.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ops-ds-hero-side">
            <div class="ops-ds-poll">
                <div class="ops-ds-poll-now">
                    @(lastPolledAt.HasValue ? $"Polled {lastPolledAt:HH:mm:ss}" : "Not polled yet")
                </div>
                <div class="ops-ds-poll-then">
                    @(report != null ? $"Report generated {report.GeneratedAt.AddHours(2):dd MMM, HH:mm}" : "No report yet")
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="ops-hero-actions">
                @* Posting used to stand here too, appearing only while something was selected. It is
                   in the selection bar above the table now, where the design puts it: the count is
                   the whole point of that button, and it is worth more directly above the rows it
                   names than four feet away at the top of the page. *@
                <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-primary" @onclick="OpenConsolidate"
                        disabled="@(isConsolidating || isLoading)">
                    @if (isConsolidating)
                    {
                        <span class="ops-spin"></span>
                        <span>Consolidating&hellip;</span>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <i class="ph ph-paper-plane-tilt" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        <span>Consolidate day</span>
                    }
                </button>
                @* The same sales, summed: by payment method, day, hour, shop and item. *@
                <a class="ops-btn ops-btn-ghost" href="/reports/desktop-sales">
                    <i class="ph ph-chart-pie-slice" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    <span>Sales analysis</span>
                </a>
                <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-ghost" @onclick="ExportToExcel"
                        disabled="@(isExporting || sales.Count == 0)">
                    @if (isExporting)
                    {
                        <span class="ops-spin"></span>
                        <span>Exporting&hellip;</span>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <i class="ph ph-file-xls" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        <span>Export</span>
                    }
                </button>
                <button type="button" class="ops-icon-btn" title="Refresh" aria-label="Refresh"
                        @onclick="RefreshData" disabled="@isLoading">
                    @if (isLoading)
                    {
                        <span class="ops-spin"></span>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <i class="ph ph-arrows-clockwise" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    }
                </button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ops-rule-fade"></div>

    @if (report != null)
    {
        <div class="ops-ds-summary">
            @* The day, divided. The strip states the split before the four
               numbers do, so the shape of the day is readable before a single
               figure is; each lane and its share of the strip read the same
               family, so a lane cannot be one colour and its bar another. *@
            <div class="ops-ds-card">
                <div class="ops-ds-card-head">
                    <span class="ops-ds-card-label">@dayLabel</span>
                    <span class="ops-ds-card-tally">
                        <b>@report.TotalSalesCount</b>
                        <span>fiscalized</span>
                    </span>
                </div>

                @if (laneTotal > 0)
                {
                    <div class="ops-ds-strip">
                        @foreach (var lane in lanes.Where(l => l.Count > 0))
                        {
                            <div class="ops-ds-strip-seg @lane.Family"
                                 style="width:@Percent(lane.Count, laneTotal)"></div>
                        }
                    </div>
                }

                <div class="ops-ds-lanes">
                    @foreach (var lane in lanes)
                    {
                        <div class="ops-ds-lane @lane.Family">
                            <div class="ops-ds-lane-num">@lane.Count</div>
                            <div class="ops-ds-lane-name">@lane.Name</div>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>

                @* Net, not the gross the day totals to: the design states the
                   three amounts side by side, and Net beside VAT that adds up
                   to something else would be read as an arithmetic error.
                   `TotalSalesAmount` is subtotal + VAT — see
                   CreateDesktopSaleHandler — so the subtraction is exact.
                   What customers handed over is not stated: it includes the
                   change, and read beside Takings it looks like a mismatch. *@
                <div class="ops-ds-aside ops-ds-aside-ruled">
                    <span>Net <b>@Money(report.TotalSalesAmount - report.TotalVatAmount)</b></span>
                    <span>VAT <b>@Money(report.TotalVatAmount)</b></span>
                    <span>Takings <b>@Money(report.TotalSalesAmount)</b></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            @* The headline is what has *not* reached the back office yet, not
               the day's total — that number is the one the Consolidate button
               acts on, and the day's total is stated beside the lanes. *@
            <div class="ops-ds-card">
                <div class="ops-ds-card-head">
                    <i class="ph ph-hard-drives" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    <span class="ops-ds-card-label">Value on the device</span>
                </div>

                <div class="ops-ds-value">
                    <b>@Money(awaitingValue)</b>
                </div>
                <div class="ops-ds-aside">
                    <span>not yet in the back office · @report.UnpostedInvoiceCount awaiting close</span>
                </div>

                @* The breakdown answers "made up of what?", so it lists the
                   customers whose sales are still on the device — not the day's
                   largest, which would contradict the figure above it. *@
                <div class="ops-ds-split"></div>
                <div class="ops-ds-breakdown">
                    @if (topPartners.Count == 0)
                    {
                        <div class="ops-ds-empty">
                            @(report.TotalSalesCount == 0
                                ? "No sales captured for the day."
                                : $"All {report.TotalSalesCount} of the day's sales are in the back office.")
                        </div>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <div class="ops-ds-card-label">Largest on the device</div>
                        @foreach (var partner in topPartners)
                        {
                            <div class="ops-ds-break-row">
                                <span class="ops-ds-break-code" title="@(partner.CardName ?? partner.CardCode)">
                                    @partner.CardCode
                                </span>
                                <span class="ops-ds-break-track">
                                    <span class="ops-ds-break-fill"
                                          style="width:@Percent(partner.TotalAmount, topPartnerMax)"></span>
                                </span>
                                <span class="ops-ds-break-val">@Money(partner.TotalAmount)</span>
                            </div>
                        }
                    }
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    }

    @if (notice != null)
    {
        <div class="ops-msg @noticeFamily" role="alert">
            <i class="ph @noticeIcon" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            <div class="ops-msg-body">@notice</div>
            <button type="button" class="ops-msg-x" aria-label="Dismiss" @onclick="() => notice = null">
                <i class="ph ph-x" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            </button>
        </div>
    }

    @* The filter surface, boxed. Everything down to the pills is one control
       and reads as one: the always-visible row that most of the day is all
       anybody touches, the panel behind it, and a statement of what is set. *@
    <section class="ops-panel ops-ds-fbar">
        <div class="ops-ds-fbar-row">
            @* Committed on change rather than per keystroke. Every filter on this
               page is one deliberate act that reloads on its own, and a reload per
               character would put fifty of them behind one typed reference. *@
            <div class="ops-search ops-ds-search">
                <i class="ph ph-magnifying-glass" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                <input type="search" class="ops-input" placeholder="Sale no., receipt, SAP no. or customer"
                       aria-label="Search by sale number, receipt, SAP number or customer"
                       @bind="filterSearch" @bind:after="ApplyFilters" />
            </div>

            @* Said rather than left to be inferred. A search reaches every date — see IsSearching —
               so the period beside it is not being applied, and a period control that looked live
               while deciding nothing is the one lie this row could tell. *@
            @if (IsSearching)
            {
                <span class="ops-ds-alldates">
                    <i class="ph ph-calendar-blank" aria-hidden="true"></i>every date
                </span>
            }

            @* Not a <label>: the segment is four buttons, and a label would
               hand every click to the first of them. *@
            <div class="ops-ds-seg @(IsSearching ? "is-off" : "")" role="group" aria-label="Period">
                @foreach (var option in PeriodOptions)
                {
                    <button type="button" class="ops-ds-seg-opt @(period == option.Value ? "is-on" : "")"
                            aria-pressed="@(period == option.Value)"
                            @onclick="() => SetPeriod(option.Value)">@option.Label</button>
                }
            </div>

            @if (period == PeriodCustom)
            {
                @* Not <label>s either: NocturneDateField carries a button and a
                   popup, which a label would swallow the clicks of. *@
                <div class="ops-ds-dates">
                    <NocturneDateField Id="ds-f-from" Date="filterFromDate" DateChanged="OnFromDateChanged"
                                       AriaLabel="From date" />
                    <span class="ops-ds-dates-to" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
                    <NocturneDateField Id="ds-f-to" Date="filterToDate" DateChanged="OnToDateChanged"
                                       AriaLabel="To date" />
                </div>
            }

            @* The badge carries the count whether or not anything is set, because a
               zero beside a closed panel is what says the panel holds nothing —
               which is the question somebody asks before opening it. *@
            <button type="button" class="ops-ds-ftoggle @(filterCount > 0 ? "is-on" : "")"
                    aria-expanded="@filtersOpen" aria-controls="ds-filter-panel"
                    @onclick="() => filtersOpen = !filtersOpen">
                <i class="ph ph-sliders-horizontal" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                <span>Filters</span>
                <span class="ops-ds-fcount">@filterCount</span>
                <i class="ph @(filtersOpen ? "ph-caret-up" : "ph-caret-down")" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            </button>

            @* Only once there is something to clear. The design draws it always, but a
               button that does nothing on an unfiltered page is a dead control. *@
            @if (filterCount > 0)
            {
                <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-ghost ops-btn-sm" @onclick="ClearFilters">
                    <i class="ph ph-x" aria-hidden="true"></i>Clear all
                </button>
            }
        </div>

        @if (filtersOpen)
        {
            <div class="ops-ds-fpanel" id="ds-filter-panel">
                <div class="ops-ds-fgrid">
                    @foreach (var group in ChipGroups)
                    {
                        <div class="ops-ds-fgroup">
                            <span class="ops-kicker">@group.Name</span>
                            @if (group.Chips.Count == 0)
                            {
                                @* Said rather than left blank. An empty group where four chips
                                   used to be reads as a panel that failed to load. *@
                                <span class="ops-ds-fnone">Nothing in this period</span>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                <div class="ops-ds-chips">
                                    @foreach (var chip in group.Chips)
                                    {
                                        <button type="button"
                                                class="ops-ds-chip @chip.Family @(chip.IsOn ? "is-on" : "")"
                                                aria-pressed="@chip.IsOn"
                                                @onclick="() => ToggleChip(group.Key, chip.Value)">
                                            @if (chip.Family is not null)
                                            {
                                                <span class="ops-dot"></span>
                                            }
                                            <span class="ops-ds-chip-label">@chip.Label</span>
                                            <span class="ops-ds-chip-count">@chip.Count</span>
                                        </button>
                                    }
                                </div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    }

                    <div class="ops-ds-fgroup">
                        <label class="ops-kicker" for="ds-customer">Customer card code</label>
                        @* The API matches this one exactly, so the placeholder shows a whole code
                           rather than promising the partial match the search box above does. *@
                        <input id="ds-customer" type="search" class="ops-input ops-ds-finput"
                               placeholder="e.g. CIS006" @bind="filterCardCode" @bind:after="ApplyFilters" />
                    </div>

                    <div class="ops-ds-fgroup">
                        <span class="ops-kicker" id="ds-amount-label">Invoice total</span>
                        <div class="ops-ds-frange" role="group" aria-labelledby="ds-amount-label">
                            <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" class="ops-input ops-ds-finput"
                                   placeholder="Min" aria-label="Smallest invoice total"
                                   @bind="amountMin" @bind:after="ApplyFilters" />
                            <span class="ops-ds-frange-dash" aria-hidden="true">&ndash;</span>
                            <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" class="ops-input ops-ds-finput"
                                   placeholder="Max" aria-label="Largest invoice total"
                                   @bind="amountMax" @bind:after="ApplyFilters" />
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    @* What the drawer took against what the invoice says. A till rounds cash up, so
                       "overpaid" is the change the customer was handed and is normal; "underpaid"
                       is money the day is short, which is the one anybody comes looking for. *@
                    <div class="ops-ds-fgroup">
                        <span class="ops-kicker" id="ds-paid-label">Payment difference</span>
                        <div class="ops-ds-seg ops-ds-seg-sm" role="group" aria-labelledby="ds-paid-label">
                            @foreach (var option in PaymentDifferenceOptions)
                            {
                                <button type="button"
                                        class="ops-ds-seg-opt @(paidCompare == option.Value ? "is-on" : "")"
                                        aria-pressed="@(paidCompare == option.Value)"
                                        @onclick="() => SetPaidCompare(option.Value)">@option.Label</button>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        }

        @* What is set, in one line, whether or not the panel is open. It is the
           answer to "why am I looking at eleven rows" on a page whose filters
           are three clicks deep, and every pill removes the one filter it names. *@
        @if (activeFilters.Count > 0)
        {
            <div class="ops-ds-active">
                <span class="ops-kicker">Active</span>
                @foreach (var pill in activeFilters)
                {
                    <button type="button" class="ops-ds-pill" @onclick="pill.Remove"
                            aria-label="@($"Remove the {pill.Group} filter {pill.Label}")">
                        <span class="ops-ds-pill-group">@pill.Group</span>
                        <span>@pill.Label</span>
                        <i class="ph ph-x" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    </button>
                }
            </div>
        }
    </section>

    @* The count and the two list controls, between the filters that decide the
       count and the table it describes. "Filtered from" is stated only when the
       panel actually took something out, so the line does not carry a number
       that is always the same as the one beside it. *@
    <div class="ops-ds-listbar">
        <div class="ops-ds-listbar-count">
            <span class="ops-ds-shown">@shownText</span>
            @if (unfilteredCount > totalCount)
            {
                <span class="ops-ds-from">filtered from @unfilteredCount</span>
            }
        </div>

        <div class="ops-ds-listbar-tools">
            <span class="ops-label" id="ds-sort-label">Sort</span>
            <NocturneSelect Class="nsel-sm nsel-auto" TValue="string" Options="SortOptions"
                            @bind-Value="sort" OnChanged="ApplyFilters"
                            Caption="Sort the list" AriaLabel="Sort the list" />

            <span class="ops-label" id="ds-rows-label">Rows</span>
            @* Right-anchored: it is the last control on the row, and a menu that
               opened leftwards from here runs off the edge on a narrow window. *@
            <NocturneSelect Class="nsel-sm nsel-auto nsel-end" TValue="int" Options="PageSizeOptions"
                            Value="pageSize" ValueChanged="@(size => SetPageSize(size))"
                            Caption="Rows per page" AriaLabel="Rows per page" />
        </div>
    </div>

    @* Only while something is ticked. A permanently visible "Post 0 to SAP" would
       invite the press that does nothing, and the count is the whole point of the
       button: it states what is about to reach SAP before it does. *@
    @if (selected.Count > 0)
    {
        <div class="ops-ds-selbar">
            <span class="ops-ds-selbar-count">@selected.Count selected</span>
            <span class="ops-ds-selbar-value">@Money(selectedValue)</span>
            <span class="ops-ds-selbar-gap"></span>
            <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-primary ops-btn-sm" @onclick="OpenBulkPost"
                    disabled="@(isPosting || isLoading)">
                @if (isPosting)
                {
                    <span class="ops-spin"></span>
                    <span>Posting&hellip;</span>
                }
                else
                {
                    <i class="ph ph-cloud-arrow-up" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    <span>Post @selected.Count to SAP</span>
                }
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-ghost ops-btn-sm" @onclick="() => selected.Clear()">
                Clear
            </button>
        </div>
    }

    @if (isLoading)
    {
        <div class="ops-loading">
            <span class="ops-spin ops-spin-lg"></span>
            <p>Loading desktop sales&hellip;</p>
        </div>
    }
    else if (sales.Count > 0)
    {
        <div class="ops-panel ops-ds-list">
            <div class="ops-scroll">
                <table class="ops-table mobile-card-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th class="ops-ds-pick">
                                @* Selects the postable rows on this page, not every row that
                                   matches the filters. The button it feeds names a count, and a
                                   count that included rows the operator cannot see would be the
                                   one number on the page they could not check. *@
                                @if (postableOnPage.Count > 0)
                                {
                                    <input type="checkbox" aria-label="Select every postable sale on this page"
                                           title="Select every postable sale on this page"
                                           checked="@allPostableSelected" @onchange="ToggleSelectAll" />
                                }
                            </th>
                            <th>Sale</th>
                            <th>Customer</th>
                            <th>Warehouse</th>
                            <th>Fiscalized</th>
                            <th class="ops-num">Total</th>
                            <th class="ops-num">Paid</th>
                            <th>Payment</th>
                            <th>Fiscal</th>
                            <th>Consolidation</th>
                            @* Empty on purpose: the mark below is a hover cue
                               for a row that already opens the drawer, not a
                               column of data. *@
                            <th class="ops-ds-chev-cell"><span class="visually-hidden">Open</span></th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var sale in sales)
                        {
                            <tr @onclick="() => SelectSale(sale)"
                                class="@RowClass(sale)">
                                @* The cell stops the click rather than the input: a press anywhere
                                   in the gutter should tick the box, and letting either reach the
                                   row would open the drawer over the list being selected. *@
                                <td class="ops-ds-pick" data-label="Select"
                                    @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
                                    @if (sale.CanPostToSap)
                                    {
                                        <input type="checkbox" aria-label="@($"Select sale {sale.Number} for posting")"
                                               checked="@selected.Contains(sale.ExternalReferenceId)"
                                               @onchange="e => ToggleSelected(sale, e)" />
                                    }
                                    else
                                    {
                                        @* Not an empty cell: a row with no box needs to say why, or
                                           it reads as a rendering fault. *@
                                        <span class="ops-ds-nopick" title="@sale.PostRefusal" aria-label="@sale.PostRefusal">
                                            <i class="ph ph-minus" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                                        </span>
                                    }
                                </td>
                                @* The short number leads and the device reference sits under it. The
                                   reference is still here, and still in full, because fiscal and support
                                   work is keyed on it — but it is not what a person picking a sale to
                                   credit should have to read, which is the whole reason the number above
                                   it exists. It is also what the till printed on the customer's receipt,
                                   so a return is matched by reading one short string off the paper. *@
                                <td data-label="Sale">
                                    <span class="ops-ds-saleno">@sale.Number</span>
                                    <div class="ops-sub ops-code ops-ds-devref">@sale.ExternalReferenceId</div>
                                    @if (sale.SapDocNum.HasValue)
                                    {
                                        <div class="ops-sub">SAP #@sale.SapDocNum</div>
                                    }
                                    else if (HeldUntil(sale) is { } heldUntil)
                                    {
                                        <div class="ops-sub ops-ds-held" title="@sale.LastPostingError">
                                            Resends after @Clock(heldUntil)
                                        </div>
                                    }
                                    else if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(sale.LastPostingError))
                                    {
                                        <div class="ops-sub ops-ds-rejected" title="@sale.LastPostingError">
                                            SAP refused it@(sale.PostingAttempts > 0 ? $" · {sale.PostingAttempts} attempts" : "")
                                        </div>
                                    }
                                </td>
                                <td data-label="Customer" class="ops-name">
                                    @(sale.CardName ?? sale.CardCode)
                                    @if (sale.CardName != null)
                                    {
                                        <span class="ops-sub"> · @sale.CardCode</span>
                                    }
                                </td>
                                <td data-label="Warehouse">@sale.WarehouseCode</td>
                                <td data-label="Fiscalized" class="ops-ds-when">@Stamp(sale.CreatedAt)</td>
                                <td data-label="Total" class="ops-num">@Money(sale.TotalAmount, sale.Currency)</td>
                                <td data-label="Paid" class="ops-num">@Money(sale.AmountPaid, sale.Currency)</td>
                                @* How it was paid, with the wallet confirmation or slip number under
                                   it — the reference is what an EcoCash sale is reconciled by, so it
                                   belongs on the row rather than only in the drawer. *@
                                <td data-label="Payment">
                                    @TenderName(sale.PaymentMethod)
                                    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(sale.PaymentReference))
                                    {
                                        <div class="ops-sub">@sale.PaymentReference</div>
                                    }
                                </td>
                                <td data-label="Fiscal">
                                    <span class="ops-ds-state @FiscalFamily(sale.FiscalizationStatus)">
                                        <span class="ops-dot"></span>@Blank(sale.FiscalizationStatus)
                                    </span>
                                </td>
                                <td data-label="Consolidation">
                                    <span class="ops-ds-state @ConsolidationFamily(sale.ConsolidationStatus)">
                                        <span class="ops-dot"></span>@ConsolidationLabel(sale.ConsolidationStatus)
                                    </span>
                                    @if (sale.ConsolidationId.HasValue)
                                    {
                                        <div class="ops-sub">Run #@sale.ConsolidationId</div>
                                    }
                                </td>
                                <td class="ops-ds-chev-cell">
                                    <i class="ph ph-arrow-right ops-ds-chev" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                                </td>
                            </tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="ops-ds-foot">
                <span class="ops-ds-foot-info">@footerText</span>
                @if (totalPages > 1)
                {
                    <div class="ops-pages">
                        <button type="button" class="ops-step" disabled="@(currentPage == 1)" @onclick="PreviousPage">
                            <i class="ph ph-caret-left" aria-hidden="true"></i>Previous
                        </button>
                        <span class="ops-page-now">@currentPage of @totalPages</span>
                        <button type="button" class="ops-step" disabled="@(!hasMore)" @onclick="NextPage">
                            Next<i class="ph ph-caret-right" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        </button>
                    </div>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        @* In the same box the table would have been in, so a filter that empties
           the list does not also collapse the page's last shape. *@
        <div class="ops-panel ops-ds-list">
            <div class="ops-empty">
                <i class="ph ph-receipt" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                <h3>@emptyTitle</h3>
                <p>@emptyBody</p>
                @if (filterCount > 0)
                {
                    <div class="ops-empty-actions">
                        <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-primary" @onclick="ClearFilters">
                            <i class="ph ph-x-circle" aria-hidden="true"></i>Clear all filters
                        </button>
                    </div>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    }
</div>

@* Both overlays are siblings of `.ops`, not children — see operations.css's
   header for why they carry `.ops-overlay` themselves. *@
@if (showConsolidate)
{
    <div class="ops-overlay ops-overlay-center" @onclick="CloseConsolidate">
        <div class="ops-dialog" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="Consolidate the day"
             @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
            <div class="ops-dialog-head">
                <h2>Consolidate the day</h2>
                <button type="button" class="ops-x" aria-label="Close" @onclick="CloseConsolidate">
                    <i class="ph ph-x" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                </button>
            </div>

            <div class="ops-dialog-body">
                <p class="ops-ds-lede">
                    Everything still awaiting close is uploaded to the back office as one
                    consolidation document. Fiscal numbers stay as they were issued on the device.
                </p>

                <dl class="ops-ds-facts">
                    <dt>Awaiting close</dt>
                    <dd>@(report?.UnpostedInvoiceCount ?? 0)</dd>
                    <dt>Value listed</dt>
                    <dd>@Money(awaitingValue)</dd>
                    <dt>Already consolidated</dt>
                    <dd>@consolidatedCount</dd>
                    <dt>Posting date</dt>
                    <dd>@(consolidationDate?.ToString("dd.MM.yyyy") ?? "—")</dd>
                </dl>

                @* The run closes the day these figures are for. It used to close today whatever the
                   page showed, so yesterday's leftovers could never be reached from here and the button
                   answered "could not be started". *@
                <div class="ops-msg ops-fam-neutral">
                    <i class="ph ph-info" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    <div class="ops-msg-body">
                        Only desktop-app sales are consolidated. Till, vending and van sales post to SAP
                        one invoice each: to push those through, select them in the list and use
                        Post to SAP.
                    </div>
                </div>

                @if (consolidationDate is { } day && day > DateTime.Today)
                {
                    <div class="ops-msg ops-fam-warn">
                        <i class="ph ph-warning" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        <div class="ops-msg-body">
                            @day.ToString("dd MMM yyyy") has not happened yet, so there is nothing to close.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                }
            </div>

            <div class="ops-dialog-foot">
                <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-ghost" @onclick="CloseConsolidate">Cancel</button>
                <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-primary" @onclick="TriggerConsolidation"
                        disabled="@(isConsolidating || !canConsolidate)">
                    @if (isConsolidating)
                    {
                        <span class="ops-spin"></span>
                        <span>Consolidating&hellip;</span>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <span>Consolidate</span>
                    }
                </button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
}

@if (selectedSale != null)
{
    var fiscalQrImageSrc = FiscalQrCode.ToImageSource(selectedSale.FiscalQRCode);

    <div class="ops-overlay ops-overlay-right" @onclick="CloseDrawer">
        <aside class="ops-drawer ops-ds-drawer" role="dialog" aria-modal="true"
               aria-label="Sale @selectedSale.Number"
               @onclick:stopPropagation="true">

            @* The head states the document rather than the page: a kicker, the sale's own short number
               as the heading, and the two statuses as marks. The device reference follows it in full
               and wraps rather than being truncated — it is the string support and ZIMRA work is keyed
               on, and a truncated one matches nothing. *@
            <div class="ops-drawer-head ops-ds-dhead">
                <div class="ops-ds-dhead-id">
                    <span class="ops-kicker">Fiscalized invoice</span>
                    <span class="ops-ds-dhead-no">@selectedSale.Number</span>
                    <span class="ops-ds-dhead-ref">@selectedSale.ExternalReferenceId</span>
                </div>

                @* Up and down walk the page that is loaded, not the whole
                   result set — paging is what the pager is for, and a Next
                   that silently fetched the next page would move the list
                   under the user while they were reading one row of it. *@
                <div class="ops-ds-dnav">
                    <button type="button" class="ops-icon-btn" title="Previous sale"
                            aria-label="Previous sale" disabled="@(sales.Count < 2)"
                            @onclick="() => StepSale(-1)">
                        <i class="ph ph-caret-up" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    </button>
                    <button type="button" class="ops-icon-btn" title="Next sale"
                            aria-label="Next sale" disabled="@(sales.Count < 2)"
                            @onclick="() => StepSale(1)">
                        <i class="ph ph-caret-down" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    </button>
                    <button type="button" class="ops-icon-btn" title="Close"
                            aria-label="Close" @onclick="CloseDrawer">
                        <i class="ph ph-x" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    </button>
                </div>

                <div class="ops-ds-dtags">
                    <span class="ops-badge @ConsolidationFamily(selectedSale.ConsolidationStatus)">
                        @ConsolidationLabel(selectedSale.ConsolidationStatus)
                    </span>
                    <span class="ops-ds-state @FiscalFamily(selectedSale.FiscalizationStatus)">
                        <span class="ops-dot"></span>Fiscal @Blank(selectedSale.FiscalizationStatus)
                    </span>
                    @* One timestamp, stated once. The design shows a raised
                       time and a fiscalized time; a desktop sale carries
                       CreatedAt and nothing else, and two labels over one
                       value would invent a gap nobody measured. *@
                    <span class="ops-ds-dwhen">@Full(selectedSale.CreatedAt)</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="ops-drawer-body">
                <div class="ops-ds-headline">
                    <b>@Money(selectedSale.TotalAmount, selectedSale.Currency)</b>
                    <span>@tenderText</span>
                </div>

                @* SAP's own words about why it refused, verbatim. Shown whenever there is one and
                   no invoice, which is exactly when somebody has opened this drawer to find out
                   what to do — an operator cannot fix "posting failed". It sits above the facts
                   rather than in the trail below them: the trail states which step a sale stopped
                   at, and a paragraph of SAP table names inside a timeline is unreadable. *@
                @* A hold is not a refusal. A post went out, the answer was lost, and the API will not
                   send the sale again until SAP has had time to show an invoice it may already have
                   taken. Shown under "SAP has not accepted this sale" it sent people into SAP to hunt
                   for a document the posting run was simply waiting to send. What the post failed
                   with still goes under it, because that is what says whether it will go through. *@
                @if (HeldUntil(selectedSale) is { } drawerHeldUntil)
                {
                    <div class="ops-msg ops-fam-warn" role="status">
                        <i class="ph ph-hourglass-medium" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        <div class="ops-msg-body">
                            <strong>Waiting to send this sale to SAP again.</strong>
                            <div>
                                A post went to SAP without a clear answer, so SAP may already hold the
                                invoice. It is sent again after @Clock(drawerHeldUntil), once SAP has
                                been asked first, so it cannot be invoiced twice.
                            </div>
                            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(selectedSale.LastPostingError))
                            {
                                <div class="ops-ds-sapsaid">@selectedSale.LastPostingError</div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>
                }
                else if (!selectedSale.SapDocNum.HasValue && !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(selectedSale.LastPostingError))
                {
                    <div class="ops-msg ops-fam-bad" role="alert">
                        <i class="ph ph-warning-octagon" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        <div class="ops-msg-body">
                            <strong>SAP has not accepted this sale.</strong>
                            <div class="ops-ds-sapsaid">@selectedSale.LastPostingError</div>
                            @if (selectedSale.PostingAttempts > 0)
                            {
                                <div class="ops-sub">
                                    @selectedSale.PostingAttempts attempt@(selectedSale.PostingAttempts == 1 ? "" : "s") so far.
                                </div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>
                }

                <div class="ops-defs">
                    <div>
                        <div class="ops-def-k">Customer</div>
                        <div class="ops-def-v">@(selectedSale.CardName ?? selectedSale.CardCode)</div>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <div class="ops-def-k">Card code</div>
                        <div class="ops-def-v">@selectedSale.CardCode</div>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <div class="ops-def-k">Warehouse</div>
                        <div class="ops-def-v">@selectedSale.WarehouseCode</div>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <div class="ops-def-k">Raised in</div>
                        <div class="ops-def-v">@Blank(selectedSale.SourceSystem)</div>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <div class="ops-def-k">Captured by</div>
                        <div class="ops-def-v">
                            @CapturedBy(selectedSale)
                            @if (UnresolvedAccount(selectedSale) is { Length: > 0 } account)
                            {
                                <div class="ops-sub ops-code">@account</div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <div class="ops-def-k">Payment method</div>
                        <div class="ops-def-v">@Blank(selectedSale.PaymentMethod)</div>
                    </div>
                    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(selectedSale.PaymentReference))
                    {
                        <div>
                            <div class="ops-def-k">Payment reference</div>
                            <div class="ops-def-v">@selectedSale.PaymentReference</div>
                        </div>
                    }
                    @* What this sale became in SAP, as a fact rather than only as a line in the
                       trail — it is the number somebody opens this drawer to copy, and a timeline
                       entry is not where you go to copy a number.

                       Two routes reach SAP and they write different columns, so both are stated
                       and neither is left to be inferred from the other's absence. A blank SAP
                       invoice on a consolidated sale does not mean it never got there; it means
                       the run's invoice carries it, which is what the sub-line says. *@
                    @if (selectedSale.SapDocNum.HasValue)
                    {
                        <div>
                            <div class="ops-def-k">SAP invoice</div>
                            <div class="ops-def-v">#@selectedSale.SapDocNum</div>
                        </div>
                    }
                    @if (selectedSale.ConsolidationId.HasValue)
                    {
                        <div>
                            <div class="ops-def-k">Consolidation run</div>
                            <div class="ops-def-v">
                                #@selectedSale.ConsolidationId
                                @if (!selectedSale.SapDocNum.HasValue)
                                {
                                    <div class="ops-sub">carries the SAP invoice for this sale</div>
                                }
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                    @if (selectedSale.PostedAt.HasValue)
                    {
                        <div>
                            <div class="ops-def-k">Posted</div>
                            <div class="ops-def-v">@Full(selectedSale.PostedAt.Value)</div>
                        </div>
                    }
                    @* Stated only once the invoice exists. Before that "not attempted" is not news
                       — there is nothing for a payment to settle. *@
                    @if (selectedSale.SapDocNum.HasValue)
                    {
                        <div>
                            <div class="ops-def-k">Settlement</div>
                            <div class="ops-def-v">
                                <span class="ops-ds-state @PaymentFamily(selectedSale.PaymentStatus)">
                                    <span class="ops-dot"></span>@PaymentLabel(selectedSale.PaymentStatus)
                                </span>
                                @if (selectedSale.PaymentSapDocNum.HasValue)
                                {
                                    <div class="ops-sub">Payment #@selectedSale.PaymentSapDocNum</div>
                                }
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>

                @if (selectedSale.Lines.Count > 0)
                {
                    <div class="ops-ds-block">
                        <div class="ops-def-k">Lines</div>
                        <table class="ops-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Item</th>
                                    <th class="ops-num">Qty</th>
                                    <th class="ops-num">Unit price</th>
                                    <th class="ops-num">Total</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                @foreach (var line in selectedSale.Lines)
                                {
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>
                                            <span class="ops-code">@line.ItemCode</span>
                                            @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(line.ItemDescription))
                                            {
                                                <div class="ops-sub">@line.ItemDescription</div>
                                            }
                                        </td>
                                        <td class="ops-num">@QuantityDisplay.Format(line.Quantity)</td>
                                        <td class="ops-num">@line.UnitPrice.ToString("N2")</td>
                                        <td class="ops-num">@line.LineTotal.ToString("N2")</td>
                                    </tr>
                                }
                            </tbody>
                        </table>

                        @* Just "VAT": the design labels it 13%, but the rate is
                           charged per line at its own tax code's rate — see
                           CreateDesktopSaleHandler — so a basket carrying a
                           zero-rated item is not 13% of anything. *@
                        <div class="ops-ds-sum">
                            <div>
                                <span>Net</span>
                                <span>@Money(selectedSale.TotalAmount - selectedSale.VatAmount, selectedSale.Currency)</span>
                            </div>
                            <div>
                                <span>VAT</span>
                                <span>@Money(selectedSale.VatAmount, selectedSale.Currency)</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="ops-ds-sum-total">
                                <span>Total</span>
                                <span>@Money(selectedSale.TotalAmount, selectedSale.Currency)</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                }

                @* What has been given back against this sale.

                   Read when the drawer opens rather than carried on the list row: a credit is rare,
                   the list is a page of fifty, and the endpoint that answers this is the same one
                   the credit dialog reads. Only fiscalised sales are asked about — nothing else can
                   have been credited, because a credit is raised against the original receipt.

                   The dialog behind the footer button is where credits are raised and chased. This
                   states that they exist, which is the thing somebody reading a sale needs to know
                   before they read its total as money the business kept. *@
                @if (selectedSale.FiscalizationStatus is "Success" && (drawerCreditsBusy || drawerCreditsError != null || drawerCredits.Count > 0))
                {
                    <div class="ops-ds-block">
                        <div class="ops-def-k">Credit notes</div>

                        @if (drawerCreditsBusy)
                        {
                            <div class="ops-sub"><span class="ops-spin"></span> Reading credit notes&hellip;</div>
                        }
                        else if (drawerCreditsError != null)
                        {
                            <div class="ops-sub">@drawerCreditsError</div>
                            <div>
                                <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-ghost ops-btn-sm"
                                        @onclick="LoadDrawerCredits">Try again</button>
                            </div>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <div class="ops-ds-credits">
                                @foreach (var note in drawerCredits)
                                {
                                    <div class="ops-ds-credit">
                                        <div class="ops-ds-credit-top">
                                            <span class="ops-code" title="@note.Number">@note.DisplayNumber</span>
                                            <span class="ops-ds-credit-amt">@Money(note.Amount, note.Currency)</span>
                                        </div>
                                        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(note.Reason))
                                        {
                                            <div class="ops-sub">@note.Reason</div>
                                        }
                                        <div class="ops-ds-state @DesktopCreditLabels.Family(note.Status)">
                                            <span class="ops-dot"></span>@DesktopCreditLabels.Fiscal(note.Status)
                                        </div>
                                        <div class="ops-sub">SAP: @DesktopCreditLabels.Sap(note) · raised @Full(note.CreatedAtUtc)</div>
                                        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(note.SapError))
                                        {
                                            <div class="ops-sub">@note.SapError</div>
                                        }
                                    </div>
                                }
                            </div>

                            @* Only what ZIMRA holds is taken off the total. A credit that was refused
                               gave nothing back, and one still being submitted has not yet. *@
                            @if (CreditedAmount > 0)
                            {
                                <div class="ops-ds-sum">
                                    <div>
                                        <span>Credited</span>
                                        <span>@Money(CreditedAmount, selectedSale.Currency)</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <div class="ops-ds-sum-total">
                                        <span>Net of credits</span>
                                        <span>@Money(selectedSale.TotalAmount - CreditedAmount, selectedSale.Currency)</span>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            }
                        }
                    </div>
                }

                @* The receipt the device issued, which lives on the sale and nowhere else: it was
                   signed under this external reference rather than a SAP document number, so the
                   invoice this sale becomes carries no QR to fall back on.

                   This is where the design's "fiscal signature" panel landed. The design draws the
                   signature as a line of text; the code is a URL, so it is drawn as the scannable
                   thing it is and the text beside it carries what a phone call needs — the
                   verification code in the blocks of four the receipt prints, and the same URL as
                   a link for anyone reading this at a desk. *@
                @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(fiscalQrImageSrc))
                {
                    <div class="ops-ds-qr">
                        <div class="ops-ds-qr-plate">
                            <img src="@fiscalQrImageSrc" alt="ZIMRA fiscal receipt QR code" />
                        </div>
                        <div class="ops-ds-qr-copy">
                            <div class="ops-def-k">Fiscal signature</div>
                            <div class="ops-ds-qr-title">Scan to verify this fiscal receipt</div>
                            <p>
                                Issued on the device for @selectedSale.ExternalReferenceId@(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(selectedSale.FiscalDeviceNumber)
                                    ? "" : $" · device {selectedSale.FiscalDeviceNumber}")@(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(selectedSale.FiscalDayNo)
                                    ? "" : $" · fiscal day {selectedSale.FiscalDayNo}").
                            </p>
                            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(selectedSale.FiscalVerificationCode))
                            {
                                <div class="ops-ds-qr-code">
                                    <span class="ops-def-k">Verification code</span>
                                    @* Grouped in fours, the way the receipt prints it and the way
                                       FiscalReceiptQrComposer.FormatVerificationCode renders it on
                                       the API side. Display only — ZIMRA is sent it unbroken. *@
                                    <span class="ops-code">@FiscalQrCode.FormatVerificationCode(selectedSale.FiscalVerificationCode)</span>
                                </div>
                            }
                            @if (verifyUrl != null)
                            {
                                <div class="ops-ds-qr-code">
                                    <a class="ops-ds-verify" href="@verifyUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                                        Verify with ZIMRA<i class="ph ph-arrow-up-right" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                                    </a>
                                </div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>
                }

                <div class="ops-ds-block">
                    <div class="ops-def-k">Consolidation trail</div>
                    <div class="ops-ds-trail">
                        @foreach (var step in trail)
                        {
                            <div class="ops-ds-trail-step @step.Family">
                                <div class="ops-ds-trail-rail">
                                    <span class="ops-ds-trail-mark @(step.IsNow ? "is-now" : "")"></span>
                                </div>
                                <div class="ops-ds-trail-body">
                                    <div class="ops-ds-trail-label">@step.Label</div>
                                    <div class="ops-ds-trail-meta">@step.Meta</div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            @* The design draws "Consolidate now", "Exclude from close" and "Receipt PDF" here.
               Post to SAP is not any of the three, and is deliberately not labelled as if it
               were: posting sends this one sale to SAP as its own invoice, which is what
               `POST sales/{reference}/post` does, while consolidating is the end-of-day run that
               folds a whole day into one invoice per customer. There is still no endpoint that
               excludes a sale from the close, and no receipt PDF is kept for one. *@
            <div class="ops-drawer-foot">
                <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-ghost" @onclick="CloseDrawer">Close</button>
                @if (string.Equals(selectedSale.FiscalizationStatus, "Success", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
                {
                    <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-primary" @onclick="OpenDesktopCredit">
                        <i class="ph ph-arrow-u-up-left" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        Credit notes
                    </button>
                }
                @if (selectedSale.CanRetryFiscalisation)
                {
                    @* Offered only where the API says a retry is allowed. The API asks the device for an
                       existing receipt before sending anything, so pressing it twice cannot sign twice. *@
                    <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-primary"
                            @onclick="() => RetryFiscalisation(selectedSale)"
                            disabled="@(isRetryingFiscal || isPosting || isLoading)">
                        @if (isRetryingFiscal)
                        {
                            <span class="ops-spin"></span>
                            <span>Fiscalising&hellip;</span>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <i class="ph ph-arrow-clockwise" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                            <span>Retry fiscalisation</span>
                        }
                    </button>
                }
                @if (selectedSale.CanPostToSap)
                {
                    <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-primary"
                            @onclick="() => PostOne(selectedSale)"
                            disabled="@(isPosting || isLoading)">
                        @if (postingReference == selectedSale.ExternalReferenceId)
                        {
                            <span class="ops-spin"></span>
                            <span>Posting&hellip;</span>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <i class="ph ph-cloud-arrow-up" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                            <span>Post to SAP</span>
                        }
                    </button>
                }
                else if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(selectedSale.PostRefusal) && !selectedSale.CanRetryFiscalisation)
                {
                    @* The reason stands in the button's place rather than a disabled button
                       standing there greyed out with nothing to explain it. *@
                    <span class="ops-ds-foot-why">@selectedSale.PostRefusal</span>
                }
            </div>
        </aside>
    </div>
}

@* Confirmed before it runs, like the consolidation is, and for a stronger reason: a SAP invoice
   cannot be withdrawn, only reversed by a credit note. The dialog states the count and the value
   about to reach SAP, because those are the two numbers somebody would want to have checked. *@
@if (showBulkPost)
{
    <div class="ops-overlay ops-overlay-center" @onclick="CloseBulkPost">
        <div class="ops-dialog" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="Post sales to SAP"
             @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
            <div class="ops-dialog-head">
                <h2>Post @selected.Count to SAP</h2>
                <button type="button" class="ops-x" aria-label="Close" @onclick="CloseBulkPost">
                    <i class="ph ph-x" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                </button>
            </div>

            <div class="ops-dialog-body">
                <p class="ops-ds-lede">
                    Each of these becomes one A/R invoice in SAP, keeping its own fiscal receipt.
                    They are sent one at a time; a sale SAP refuses does not stop the rest.
                </p>

                <dl class="ops-ds-facts">
                    <dt>Sales selected</dt>
                    <dd>@selected.Count</dd>
                    <dt>Value selected</dt>
                    <dd>@Money(selectedValue)</dd>
                    <dt>Posting date</dt>
                    <dd>@DateTime.Today.ToString("dd.MM.yyyy")</dd>
                </dl>

                <div class="ops-msg ops-fam-info">
                    <i class="ph ph-shield-check" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    <div class="ops-msg-body">
                        Sending this twice is safe. Each sale is claimed before anything reaches SAP,
                        so a sale that has already posted is reported with the invoice it became
                        rather than being invoiced again.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="ops-dialog-foot">
                <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-ghost" @onclick="CloseBulkPost">Cancel</button>
                <button type="button" class="ops-btn ops-btn-primary" @onclick="PostSelected"
                        disabled="@isPosting">
                    @if (isPosting)
                    {
                        <span class="ops-spin"></span>
                        <span>Posting&hellip;</span>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <span>Post @selected.Count to SAP</span>
                    }
                </button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
}

@* What the batch actually did, sale by sale. A summary line alone would not do: the whole point of
   posting a set is that its members can end differently, and "4 of 5 posted" names neither the one
   that did not nor why. *@
@if (bulkResult != null)
{
